course: dictionary
lesson: 5385
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Knock is one of them. You can knock on a door, knock a cup over, or hear a knock. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it. Those words are the context, and they leave clues.
explain: «A moving swing is heavy and fast at a child's head height and can knock you out or break teeth, so walk wide around swings.» Here knock out is not about a door. The clues are heavy, fast and break teeth, so knock out means make someone unconscious.
explain: «For example, a girl in London kicks her ball over the fence; she knocks on the door with her dad and asks politely.» The word door tells you that knocks means tapping the door with your knuckles, so people inside can hear you.
explain: Small words after knock change its meaning. Knock over, knock down and knock out each mean something different. When you meet knock, look at the word that follows it, then try your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Knock can be a verb (to knock) or a noun (a knock).
fact: «Knock» means to hit a door or window with your knuckles so people inside can hear you.
fact: «Knock over» means to hit something by accident so that it falls, like «knock over a cup».
fact: «Knock out» can mean to make someone unconscious, or to beat a team so it leaves a competition.
fact: «Knock it off» means stop it, and «knock-knock jokes» are funny jokes that start with a knock.
exercise: «A moving swing can knock you out, so walk wide around swings.» What does knock out mean here? | Tap gently on a door | *Make someone unconscious | Move a chair across the room | Shout loudly to a friend
exercise: Your friend says, «I knocked my knee on the desk.» What does knocked mean? | *Hit or bumped a part of your body | Fell asleep in class | Made a bell ring | Tapped a door with your knuckles
exercise: Sam says, «Before you come into my room, knock first.» Why should you knock? | To make a joke about the door | To make the door fall down | To hit the door as hard as you can | *To let people inside know you are there, which is polite
exercise: 
test: «She knocks over her cup at breakfast.» What does knocks over mean here? | Puts the cup on a high shelf | *Hits the cup by accident so it falls | Taps the cup to say hello | Washes the cup carefully
test: Which word is a noun in this sentence? «There was a loud knock at the door.» | door | loud | was | *knock
exam: «Dad said, knock it off, you two.» What does knock it off mean here? | *Stop it | Knock on the door | Pick it up from the floor | Hit it hard
